=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No network fault exists in this topology. A direct ping from PC1 (10.1.0.100)
to PC2 (10.1.0.101) completes with 3/3 packets received and zero loss at
~1.5 ms RTT. ARP is fully resolved in both directions — PC1 holds PC2's MAC
and vice versa — confirming end-to-end L2 reachability across their shared
VLAN 10 segment.

The team's suspicion about dswa1 or a failed port is unfounded. Dswa1's
bridge ports eth1 (facing aswa2/PC1's uplink) and eth2 (facing aswa3/PC2's
uplink) are both in STP forwarding state. The only STP-blocked ports are
aswa2.eth2 and aswa3.eth2 — the secondary uplinks toward dswa2 — which is
correct, normal spanning-tree loop-prevention behavior in a redundant dual
uplink design and not a fault condition. Dswa2 itself is also fully
operational with all bridge members forwarding.

VLAN filtering is correct: the PC-facing access ports on aswa2 and aswa3 use
PVID=10 egress-untagged, and dswa1 carries VLAN 10 on all trunk members with
PC MAC entries learned in the FDB on the right ports. The iptables FORWARD
rules present on dswa1 (bridge-nf-call-iptables=1) only restrict traffic
destined to 10.3.0.0/24, leaving all 10.1.0.0/24 intra-subnet traffic
unaffected.

Root cause: the helpdesk ticket describes a fault that does not exist; the
network is healthy and PC1 can communicate with PC2 without any disruption.

Corrective action: close the ticket as a false alarm. No device restart or
configuration change is required. If the user experienced a transient issue,
it most likely self-resolved (e.g., a brief STP topology change that
reconverged before investigation began).
=== END ===
